const chapters = [
  {
    title: "Playwright foundations", symbol: "✳", lessons: [
      { title: "What is Playwright?", category: "PLAYWRIGHT FOUNDATIONS", duration: 5, summary: "Understand browser automation and where Playwright fits.", theory: "Playwright is an automation library for testing and controlling Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit browsers. The Python package offers synchronous and asynchronous APIs. It drives real browser engines, so tests can exercise rendered pages, navigation, user interactions, and network behavior.", analogy: "Playwright is a careful remote operator: it opens a real browser, performs the steps you describe, and checks what a visitor can see.", goal: "Explain what Playwright automates and name the browser engines it supports.", code: "from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright\n\nwith sync_playwright() as playwright:\n    browser = playwright.chromium.launch(headless=True)\n    page = browser.new_page()\n    page.goto(\"https://example.com\")\n    print(page.title())\n    browser.close()", output: "Launch browser → visit page → read title → close browser", quiz: { question: "Which browser engines can Playwright automate?", options: ["Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit", "Only Google Chrome", "Only browsers installed as extensions"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Python test","Instructions","your code"],["Playwright","Automation API","browser control"],["Browser","Chromium / Firefox / WebKit","real page"]], links: ["calls","drives"], caption: "Python code uses Playwright to control a real browser engine." } },
      { title: "Install Playwright for Python", category: "PLAYWRIGHT FOUNDATIONS", duration: 6, summary: "Set up the package, browser binaries, and an isolated environment.", theory: "Install the Python package in a virtual environment with pip install playwright. Then install the browser binaries with python -m playwright install; on Linux CI, install --with-deps may also install required operating-system libraries. Keep package and browser revisions compatible, and use the same environment for installation and test execution.", analogy: "The Python package is the controller; browser installation provides the browser engines it needs to launch.", goal: "Distinguish installing the Python package from downloading browser binaries.", code: "# In an activated virtual environment\npython -m pip install playwright\npython -m playwright install\n\n# Linux CI may need browser system libraries\npython -m playwright install --with-deps", output: "Install the library first, then install compatible browsers.", quiz: { question: "What does `python -m playwright install` primarily install?", options: ["Playwright browser binaries", "Your Python interpreter", "The website being tested"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["pip","Python package","API"],["playwright install","Browser binaries","engines"],["Python tests","Environment","ready"],["CI","Run tests","repeatable"]], links: ["provides","uses","runs"], caption: "The package and the browser binaries are separate setup steps." } },
      { title: "Launch, page & browser context", category: "PLAYWRIGHT FOUNDATIONS", duration: 7, summary: "Learn the Browser → BrowserContext → Page hierarchy.", theory: "A Browser is a launched browser process. A BrowserContext is an isolated browser session with its own cookies, storage, and permissions. A Page is a tab or popup inside a context. Creating a fresh context per test helps prevent state leaking between tests.", analogy: "A browser is a building, a context is a private apartment with its own keys, and each page is a room or open tab.", goal: "Describe the browser, context, and page relationship and why contexts matter.", code: "browser = playwright.chromium.launch()\ncontext = browser.new_context()\npage = context.new_page()\npage.goto(\"https://example.com\")\n\ncontext.close()\nbrowser.close()", output: "Browser process → isolated context → page tab", quiz: { question: "What does a fresh BrowserContext provide?", options: ["An isolated session for browser state", "A new Python virtual environment", "A faster locator"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Browser","Process","engine"],["Context","Isolated session","cookies + storage"],["Page","Tab","document"]], links: ["creates","contains"], caption: "Context isolation separates browser state; pages live inside a context." } },
      { title: "Your first browser test", category: "PLAYWRIGHT FOUNDATIONS", duration: 6, summary: "Write a small test that navigates, verifies, and cleans up.", theory: "A useful browser test has a clear setup, user-visible action, and assertion. With the Playwright library directly, close resources explicitly or use context managers. With pytest-playwright, request the provided page fixture and let the plugin manage browser lifecycle. Keep the tested behavior independent of implementation details.", analogy: "A test is a short user story: open the site, do one meaningful thing, then verify the result.", goal: "Recognize the arrange, act, assert structure of a browser test.", code: "from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright\n\nwith sync_playwright() as p:\n    browser = p.chromium.launch()\n    page = browser.new_page()\n    page.goto(\"https://example.com\")\n    assert page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Example Domain\").is_visible()\n    browser.close()", output: "The test passes when the expected heading is visible.", quiz: { question: "What makes a browser test meaningful?", options: ["It verifies observable behavior", "It checks internal CSS class names only", "It always waits a fixed 10 seconds"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Arrange","Open page","setup"],["Act","User action","behavior"],["Assert","Visible outcome","proof"],["Cleanup","Close resources","done"]], links: ["sets up","triggers","verifies"], caption: "Arrange the page, act like a user, and assert the observable result." } },
      { title: "Sync & async APIs", category: "PLAYWRIGHT FOUNDATIONS", duration: 7, summary: "Choose synchronous or asynchronous automation and use one consistently.", theory: "Playwright for Python provides sync_playwright and async_playwright. The synchronous API is straightforward for many scripts and pytest-playwright tests. The asynchronous API uses async/await and fits applications already built around asyncio. Do not call the synchronous API from an active asyncio event loop; choose the API that matches your runtime.", analogy: "Sync is a single clear checklist; async lets a program coordinate browser work while other tasks can make progress.", goal: "Choose the Playwright API that matches the surrounding Python runtime.", code: "import asyncio\nfrom playwright.async_api import async_playwright\n\nasync def main():\n    async with async_playwright() as p:\n        browser = await p.chromium.launch()\n        page = await browser.new_page()\n        await page.goto(\"https://example.com\")\n        print(await page.title())\n        await browser.close()\n\nasyncio.run(main())", output: "Async browser calls are awaited; the event loop runs the coroutine.", quiz: { question: "What should an asyncio-based app normally use?", options: ["Playwright's async API", "Blocking sync calls inside the event loop", "Only shell commands"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["async def","Coroutine","work"],["await","Browser call","yield"],["Event loop","Schedules","other work"],["result","Resume","continue"]], links: ["starts","pauses at","resumes"], caption: "The async API yields at awaited browser operations and resumes with results." } }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "Locators & interactions", symbol: "⌖", lessons: [
      { title: "Locators & auto-waiting", category: "LOCATORS & INTERACTIONS", duration: 7, summary: "Find elements with retryable locators instead of brittle timing.", theory: "A Locator describes how to find an element and resolves it when an operation runs. Playwright retries locator actions while waiting for the element to become actionable, reducing the need for arbitrary sleeps. Prefer locators over storing a transient element handle, especially on dynamic pages.", analogy: "A locator is a live address that Playwright can re-check; an element handle is a snapshot that may go stale after the page changes.", goal: "Explain why locators and auto-waiting are more reliable than fixed sleeps.", code: "from playwright.sync_api import expect\n\nsave_button = page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Save\")\nexpect(save_button).to_be_visible()\nsave_button.click()\nexpect(page.get_by_text(\"Saved\")).to_be_visible()", output: "The locator is resolved and retried as the page becomes ready.", quiz: { question: "Why prefer a Locator over a fixed sleep?", options: ["Locators can retry against changing page state", "Locators disable browser rendering", "A sleep checks the expected result"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Locator","Description","reusable"],["Retry","Resolve element","auto-wait"],["Action","Click","when ready"],["Outcome","Page changes","verified"]], links: ["finds","waits for","causes"], caption: "Locators resolve at action time and let Playwright wait for readiness." } },
      { title: "User-facing locators", category: "LOCATORS & INTERACTIONS", duration: 6, summary: "Locate controls by accessible role, label, text, and placeholder.", theory: "get_by_role, get_by_label, get_by_text, and get_by_placeholder target user-facing semantics. Role locators include an accessible name and often make tests both robust and accessibility-aware. Test IDs are a useful explicit contract when user-visible semantics do not uniquely identify a control.", analogy: "Choose a control by what a person calls it—its role and label—not by a fragile position in the page's markup.", goal: "Select an appropriate semantic locator for a button, field, and link.", code: "page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Continue\").click()\npage.get_by_label(\"Email address\").fill(\"learner@example.com\")\npage.get_by_placeholder(\"Search lessons\").fill(\"locators\")\npage.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Help\").click()", output: "Interact through accessible roles and visible labels.", quiz: { question: "Which locator is usually the best first choice for a button?", options: ["get_by_role(\"button\", name=...)", "page.locator(\"div:nth-child(7)\")", "A random CSS class"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["User","Accessible name","Continue"],["Role locator","button","semantic query"],["Match","Unique control","element"],["Action","click()","interaction"]], links: ["names","finds","operates"], caption: "Role and accessible name model how a user identifies a control." } },
      { title: "CSS, text & test-id locators", category: "LOCATORS & INTERACTIONS", duration: 6, summary: "Use CSS and test IDs thoughtfully when semantic locators are insufficient.", theory: "page.locator accepts CSS and other selector forms supported by Playwright. CSS is useful for relationships or attributes, but deeply nested selectors and generated classes are brittle. Configure a stable test-id attribute when needed, and use locator filtering or chaining to scope a query rather than depending on global position.", analogy: "A semantic locator describes what a user sees; a test ID is a stable backstage label; a long DOM path is a brittle map.", goal: "Pick stable CSS or test-id selectors and scope them appropriately.", code: "page.get_by_test_id(\"cart-panel\").get_by_role(\n    \"button\", name=\"Checkout\"\n).click()\n\nproduct = page.locator(\".product\").filter(\n    has_text=\"Python Basics\"\n)\nproduct.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Add\").click()", output: "Scope the locator to the matching product or test-id region.", quiz: { question: "When is a test ID a useful locator?", options: ["When a stable explicit testing contract is needed", "When you want to depend on random CSS classes", "When locating a browser process"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Region","cart-panel","scope"],["Role","Checkout","target"],["Action","click","user intent"]], links: ["narrows","finds","performs"], caption: "Chain locators to search inside a meaningful page region." } },
      { title: "Click, fill & keyboard actions", category: "LOCATORS & INTERACTIONS", duration: 7, summary: "Perform common user actions with retryable locator methods.", theory: "Use click for activation, fill to replace a field's value, and press for keyboard input. Use check and uncheck for checkboxes, select_option for native selects, and set_input_files for uploads. Prefer actions that match user intent; Playwright checks actionability before interacting.", analogy: "Choose the action that matches a real user's gesture: fill a form field, check a box, select an option, then submit.", goal: "Match common locator actions to inputs, buttons, and selection controls.", code: "page.get_by_label(\"Your name\").fill(\"Asha\")\npage.get_by_label(\"Message\").fill(\"Hello there!\")\npage.get_by_label(\"Message\").press(\"Tab\")\npage.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Send message\").click()", output: "Fill → keyboard Tab → click Send message", quiz: { question: "Which method sets a checkbox to the checked state?", options: ["check()", "fill()", "select_option()"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Name field","fill()","text"],["Message","fill()","text"],["Keyboard","press(\"Tab\")","focus next"],["Send button","click()","submit"]], links: ["enters","enters","tabs to","sends"], caption: "Fill the message, use the keyboard to move focus, then click Send." } },
      { title: "Mouse actions: hover, hold & drag-and-drop", category: "LOCATORS & INTERACTIONS", duration: 7, summary: "Hover a target, perform a held mouse gesture, and drag an item into a drop zone.", theory: "Use locator.hover() to move the pointer over an element. For a custom press-and-move gesture, move the mouse to the target, call page.mouse.down(), move to the destination, then call page.mouse.up(). For a standard draggable element, locator.drag_to(target) is the clearer high-level action. Assert the resulting page state rather than assuming the gesture succeeded.", analogy: "Move the pointer to a card, hold the mouse button, carry it to a tray, and release; drag_to() packages that familiar gesture into one locator action.", goal: "Tell hover, mouse down/up, and locator.drag_to() apart and verify a successful drop.", code: "source = page.get_by_text(\"Report card\")\ntarget = page.get_by_role(\"region\", name=\"Drop zone\")\n\n# High-level drag-and-drop\nsource.drag_to(target)\nexpect(target).to_contain_text(\"Report card\")\n\n# Custom click-and-hold gesture\nsource.hover()\npage.mouse.down()\npage.mouse.move(420, 280)\npage.mouse.up()", output: "Hover → hold → move → release → verify the drop target.", quiz: { question: "Which Playwright locator method is designed for a standard drag-and-drop?", options: ["drag_to(target)", "hover(target)", "check(target)"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Source card","hover()","pointer over item"],["Mouse held","mouse.down()","press stays active"],["Move + release","move() · up()","drop gesture"],["Drop zone","drag_to()","verify new state"]], links: ["targets","holds","moves to","receives"], caption: "A held pointer moves an item; drag_to() is the concise locator-level alternative." } },
      { title: "Assertions & expect", category: "LOCATORS & INTERACTIONS", duration: 7, summary: "Assert user-visible outcomes with Playwright's retrying assertions.", theory: "Playwright's expect assertions retry until the condition passes or the assertion timeout expires. This is ideal for asynchronous UI updates. Check visibility, text, attributes, URL, and counts with the matching assertion. Avoid reading a value once and asserting it immediately when the UI may still be updating.", analogy: "A retrying assertion keeps checking the signpost until the expected state appears—or a clear deadline is reached.", goal: "Use web-first assertions to check visible state and page navigation.", code: "from playwright.sync_api import expect\n\nexpect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Dashboard\")).to_be_visible()\nexpect(page.get_by_test_id(\"cart-count\")).to_have_text(\"2\")\nexpect(page).to_have_url(\"**/dashboard\")", output: "Each expect retries until success or its timeout.", quiz: { question: "What is the key benefit of a Playwright expect(locator) assertion?", options: ["It retries while the UI reaches the expected state", "It never times out", "It skips checking the page"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["UI update","Async change","pending"],["expect()","Retry","poll state"],["Condition","True?","check"],["Pass / timeout","Result","bounded"]], links: ["changes","observes","resolves to"], caption: "Web-first assertions retry until the condition is true or time runs out." } }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "Advanced browser interactions", symbol: "⌘", lessons: [
      { title: "Role & text locators in practice", category: "ACCESSIBLE LOCATORS", duration: 6, summary: "Target visible page content with roles, accessible names, and exact text.", theory: "Use get_by_role with the correct ARIA role and accessible name for controls such as buttons, links, headings, and checkboxes. Use get_by_text for visible non-interactive copy; exact=True avoids matching longer text that merely contains the phrase. Prefer accessible roles for interactive elements and scope repeated names with locator containers.", analogy: "Ask for a control the way a person would identify it: its job (role) and its spoken name. Use visible text when the target is content rather than a control.", goal: "Choose role or text locators and make matches specific and accessible.", code: "save = page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Save profile\", exact=True)\nwelcome = page.get_by_text(\"Welcome back\", exact=True)\n\nexpect(save).to_be_visible()\nexpect(welcome).to_be_visible()\nsave.click()", output: "Find the named button and exact visible message, then act and verify.", quiz: { question: "Which locator best identifies an interactive Save button?", options: ["get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Save profile\")", "get_by_text(\"Save\") on the whole page", "locator(\"div:nth-child(4)\")"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Page content","Save profile","accessible name"],["get_by_role()","button","semantic query"],["get_by_text()","Welcome back","visible copy"],["Action / expect","unique match","verified"]], links: ["names","locates","checks"], caption: "Use roles for controls and exact text for visible content." } },
      { title: "Tooltip & text extraction", category: "ACCESSIBLE LOCATORS", duration: 6, summary: "Reveal hover tooltips and extract visible text or an element attribute.", theory: "For a native tooltip, hover the element and read its title attribute with get_attribute(\"title\"). For a custom tooltip rendered in the page, hover first, then locate role=tooltip and use inner_text() to read user-visible copy; use text_content() only when hidden or nested text is needed. Prefer expect(locator).to_have_text() when checking asynchronous tooltip content.", analogy: "Hover opens the hint; then read the hint itself or the element's title label. Visible text and an HTML attribute are different sources.", goal: "Distinguish native title tooltips from custom tooltip elements and extract the correct text.", code: "help = page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Help\")\nhelp.hover()\n\n# Native browser tooltip text comes from the title attribute\nnative_hint = help.get_attribute(\"title\")\n\n# Custom tooltips are DOM content\ntooltip = page.get_by_role(\"tooltip\")\nexpect(tooltip).to_be_visible()\nmessage = tooltip.inner_text()", output: "Hover → read title attribute or visible role=tooltip text.", quiz: { question: "Where does a native HTML title tooltip's text come from?", options: ["The element's title attribute", "A role=tooltip node that must always exist", "The browser URL"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Help control","hover()","reveal hint"],["Native title","get_attribute()","attribute text"],["Custom tooltip","role=tooltip","visible text"],["Assertion","to_have_text()","verify copy"]], links: ["reveals","reads","or locates","checks"], caption: "Native title values are attributes; custom tooltip copy is page text." } },
      { title: "Checkboxes, radio buttons & dropdowns", category: "FORMS & CONTROLS", duration: 7, summary: "Select checkboxes, radio choices, native options, and custom dropdown items.", theory: "Use check() and uncheck() to set checkbox state idempotently, and assert with is_checked or expect(locator).to_be_checked(). Radio groups represent one choice: check the named option and assert the selected option. For native select elements use select_option(value=..., label=..., index=...); custom ARIA menus are usually buttons/listboxes and should be opened then selected by role and name.", analogy: "Checkboxes allow independent on/off choices, radios choose one answer in a group, and dropdowns choose one item from a menu.", goal: "Pick the right locator action for a checkbox, radio choice, or native/custom dropdown.", code: "terms = page.get_by_role(\"checkbox\", name=\"Accept terms\")\nterms.check()\nexpect(terms).to_be_checked()\n\npage.get_by_role(\"radio\", name=\"Express delivery\").check()\npage.get_by_label(\"Country\").select_option(label=\"Canada\")\npage.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Choose plan\").click()\npage.get_by_role(\"option\", name=\"Pro\").click()", output: "Set checkbox → select one radio → choose native and custom menu options.", quiz: { question: "How should a native HTML select be changed?", options: ["select_option(label=\"Canada\")", "click().fill(\"Canada\")", "check(\"Canada\")"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Checkbox","check()","independent on/off"],["Radio group","check()","one choice"],["Native select","select_option()","option value"],["Custom menu","role=option","visible choice"]], links: ["sets state","selects one","chooses","confirms"], caption: "Match the API to the actual form control; custom menus need their visible option." } },
      { title: "Pagination & paged results", category: "FORMS & CONTROLS", duration: 6, summary: "Move between result pages and verify the current page and items.", theory: "Pagination can use numbered buttons, Next/Previous controls, or load-more patterns. Scope the controls to their navigation region, inspect disabled or aria-current state, click the next page, and assert the expected result items with retrying assertions. For cursor-based data, verify the visible state rather than relying on an implementation-specific URL alone.", analogy: "Pagination is a book with a current page marker: move one page, then check both the page marker and the new contents.", goal: "Navigate a paginated list and assert that both its page indicator and results change.", code: "pager = page.get_by_role(\"navigation\", name=\"Pagination\")\nexpect(pager.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"1\")).to_have_attribute(\n    \"aria-current\", \"page\"\n)\npager.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Next\").click()\nexpect(pager.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"2\")).to_have_attribute(\n    \"aria-current\", \"page\"\n)\nexpect(page.get_by_role(\"listitem\")).to_have_count(10)", output: "Verify page 1 → move forward → verify page 2 and its results.", quiz: { question: "What should a pagination test assert after clicking Next?", options: ["The new current-page state and the refreshed results", "Only that the click call returned", "A fixed sleep of ten seconds"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Page 1","aria-current","current"],["Next","click()","advance"],["Page 2","aria-current","updated"],["Results","listitems","new data"]], links: ["starts at","moves to","marks","renders"], caption: "Verify the page state and its content after each pagination action." } },
      { title: "Navigate backward & forward", category: "PAGES & NAVIGATION", duration: 6, summary: "Use browser history safely and verify back/forward navigation.", theory: "Page.go_back() and page.go_forward() navigate the current tab's session history and return a response or None when no navigation occurs. Check the resulting URL and visible page state with expect assertions. These methods are distinct from clicking in-page Previous/Next controls, which update application state and may not add a history entry.", analogy: "Browser history is a trail for one tab: go back to the previous location, then forward to the location you just left.", goal: "Navigate one tab's history backward and forward and assert the destination.", code: "page.goto(\"https://example.com/start\")\npage.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Details\").click()\nexpect(page).to_have_url(\"**/details\")\n\npage.go_back()\nexpect(page).to_have_url(\"**/start\")\npage.go_forward()\nexpect(page).to_have_url(\"**/details\")", output: "One tab keeps its own history; back and forward restore prior routes.", quiz: { question: "What does page.go_back() navigate?", options: ["The current page's browser history", "The previous browser tab", "A parent iframe"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["/start","Current page","history entry"],["Details","link click","navigate"],["go_back()","previous entry","restore"],["go_forward()","next entry","restore"]], links: ["clicks","adds","returns","replays"], caption: "Back and forward navigate the current tab's history, not between tabs." } },
      { title: "Multiple tabs, windows & popups", category: "PAGES & NAVIGATION", duration: 8, summary: "Capture new pages and deliberately switch among several browser tabs.", theory: "A BrowserContext owns its Pages; context.pages lists currently open tabs. Use page.expect_popup() when an action opens a popup from a page, or context.expect_page() for a new page created elsewhere in the context, registering the wait before the trigger. To work with several known tabs, retain the returned Page references or inspect context.pages and select the intended page explicitly; avoid assuming a third tab always exists or relying on order when a named popup can be captured.", analogy: "A context is the browser window collection; each Page is a tab. Capture the new tab when it opens, then choose that Page object for the next action.", goal: "Capture a popup and switch correctly between the first, second, and third pages.", code: "pages = context.pages\n\nwith page.expect_popup() as popup_info:\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Open report\").click()\nreport = popup_info.value\n\nwith context.expect_page() as new_page_info:\n    page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Open help\").click()\nhelp_page = new_page_info.value\nhelp_page.bring_to_front()\nexpect(help_page).to_have_url(\"**/help\")", output: "Capture each opened Page, then bring the intended tab forward.", quiz: { question: "When should expect_popup() be registered?", options: ["Before the click that opens the popup", "After trying to read the popup", "Only after closing the context"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Page 1","Open report","trigger"],["Popup event","expect_popup()","capture"],["Page 2","report Page","switch"],["Page 3","help Page","bring_to_front()"]], links: ["opens","returns","switches","focuses"], caption: "Each tab is a Page; capture new pages and switch using their Page objects." } },
      { title: "Frames & nested iframes", category: "PAGES & NAVIGATION", duration: 8, summary: "Locate controls in iframes, including a third nested frame.", theory: "A Frame is a document embedded in or opened by a page. Use page.frame_locator(selector) for robust chained interactions inside frames; nested frame_locator calls scope deeper into child iframes. For direct frame inspection, page.frames lists attached frames, but indices can change, so prefer stable frame names or selectors. A third-level nested iframe can be reached by chaining three frame_locator calls.", analogy: "Frames are documents inside documents. Follow each iframe boundary in order; a control inside the third nested frame is three levels down.", goal: "Reach a control inside a nested third iframe and identify frames without fragile indexes.", code: "third_frame = (\n    page.frame_locator(\"iframe#outer\")\n    .frame_locator(\"iframe#middle\")\n    .frame_locator(\"iframe#inner\")\n)\nthird_frame.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Submit answer\").click()\n\nframe_names = [frame.name for frame in page.frames]\nexpect(page.get_by_text(\"Answer received\", exact=True)).to_be_visible()", output: "Main page → outer frame → middle frame → third nested frame → control.", quiz: { question: "How do you reach an element in a nested iframe?", options: ["Chain frame_locator() for each iframe boundary", "Use page.get_by_role() as if frames were in the main document", "Always use page.frames[2] without checking"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Main page","iframe#outer","level 1"],["Outer frame","iframe#middle","level 2"],["Middle frame","iframe#inner","level 3"],["Third frame","Submit answer","target"]], links: ["enters","nests","nests","locates"], caption: "Cross each iframe boundary with another frame_locator before locating its control." } },
      { title: "Shadow DOM components", category: "PAGES & NAVIGATION", duration: 6, summary: "Locate elements inside web components and understand closed shadow roots.", theory: "Playwright's supported locators generally pierce open Shadow DOM automatically, so role, text, CSS, and test-id locators can often find component content without special syntax. XPath does not pierce shadow roots. Closed shadow roots are not accessible through ordinary page locators; test the public user-visible behavior or provide a supported testing interface instead of bypassing the component boundary.", analogy: "An open shadow root is a component room whose door Playwright's locators can traverse; a closed root intentionally keeps its internals private.", goal: "Locate content in open shadow DOM and recognize the limits of closed roots and XPath.", code: "save = page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Save settings\")\nexpect(save).to_be_visible()\nsave.click()\n\n# Open shadow roots are traversed by supported locators.\n# XPath does not pierce shadow roots; closed roots are not exposed.\nexpect(page.get_by_text(\"Settings saved\", exact=True)).to_be_visible()", output: "Use semantic locators across open components; assert the public result.", quiz: { question: "Which statement about Playwright and Shadow DOM is correct?", options: ["Supported locators pierce open roots; XPath and closed roots have limits", "XPath always pierces open and closed roots", "Every shadow root must be manually opened with JavaScript"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Page","Custom element","component"],["Open root","Supported locator","pierces"],["Control","role=button","interact"],["Closed root","private internals","not exposed"]], links: ["contains","finds","acts on","may be"], caption: "Work through open roots with normal locators; closed roots remain encapsulated." } },
      { title: "API requests & network handling", category: "NETWORK & API", duration: 8, summary: "Call APIs directly and coordinate, inspect, or mock browser network traffic.", theory: "Use playwright.request.new_context() / APIRequestContext for direct HTTP setup and API assertions, such as creating test data before a UI test. Use page.expect_response before an action to pair a UI interaction with its network response. Use page.route to mock or modify requests for deterministic tests, and dispose a standalone request context when finished. Keep direct API checks distinct from browser-driven end-to-end behavior.", analogy: "An APIRequestContext talks directly to the service; page.expect_response listens to a browser trip; page.route intercepts that trip for a controlled response.", goal: "Choose between direct API calls, observing a browser response, and mocking a route.", code: "request = playwright.request.new_context(\n    base_url=\"https://api.example.test\"\n)\nresponse = request.post(\"/todos\", data={\"title\": \"Learn\"})\nassert response.ok\n\nwith page.expect_response(\"**/api/items\") as info:\n    page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Load items\").click()\nassert info.value.status == 200\nrequest.dispose()", output: "Create data with APIRequestContext; observe browser requests with expect_response.", quiz: { question: "Which API makes direct HTTP calls without driving the page UI?", options: ["APIRequestContext", "page.frame_locator()", "Locator.check()"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Test setup","APIRequestContext","direct HTTP"],["Browser action","expect_response()","listen"],["Service","HTTP response","status + data"],["page.route()","mock / fulfill","controlled test"]], links: ["creates data","triggers","returns","can intercept"], caption: "Direct API setup, observed browser traffic, and route mocks serve different purposes." } },
      { title: "Record & replay with codegen", category: "TOOLING & DEBUGGING", duration: 6, summary: "Record browser actions with codegen, refine the generated test, and replay it.", theory: "Use python -m playwright codegen <url> to open a browser and record interactions while generating Python code. Codegen can suggest locators, but generated scripts are a starting point: review selectors, remove unnecessary actions, add explicit assertions, and move stable behavior into tests. Re-run the reviewed script or pytest suite to replay the workflow; use traces for richer post-run inspection.", analogy: "Codegen is a recorder that drafts a route from your browser journey. A developer edits the draft into a clear, verified test before replaying it.", goal: "Record a small workflow, refine generated locators/assertions, and replay the test.", code: "# Record a browser journey and generate Python\npython -m playwright codegen https://example.com\n\n# Save the reviewed workflow as a test, then replay it\npytest -q tests/test_journey.py\n# Or run a standalone generated script\npython recorded_journey.py", output: "Record → review generated selectors → add assertions → replay.", quiz: { question: "What should you do before relying on codegen output?", options: ["Review locators and add meaningful assertions", "Treat every generated line as production-ready", "Remove all expected-result checks"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Codegen","record action","draft"],["Generated code","locators","review"],["Assertions","expected state","verify"],["Replay","pytest / script","regress"]], links: ["records","produces","strengthen","runs"], caption: "Codegen drafts a workflow; review and assert before replaying it." } }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "Tests, fixtures & browser features", symbol: "▦", lessons: [
      { title: "Pytest & pytest-playwright", category: "TESTS & FIXTURES", duration: 7, summary: "Use pytest fixtures to keep browser setup and cleanup consistent.", theory: "pytest-playwright integrates Playwright with pytest and provides fixtures such as page, context, browser, and browser_name. The plugin manages fixture lifetimes and configuration. Install it with pip install pytest-playwright; run pytest and configure options such as browser selection through pytest settings or command-line flags.", analogy: "A fixture is a prepared test workspace: pytest sets it up for a test and handles the cleanup afterward.", goal: "Describe what the page fixture provides and how pytest-playwright is used.", code: "# test_home.py\nfrom playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\ndef test_homepage_heading(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://example.com\")\n    expect(page.get_by_role(\n        \"heading\", name=\"Example Domain\"\n    )).to_be_visible()\n\n# python -m pip install pytest-playwright\n# python -m playwright install\n# pytest --browser chromium", output: "pytest creates a page fixture and closes its resources after the test.", quiz: { question: "What does the pytest-playwright `page` fixture provide?", options: ["A managed Playwright page for the test", "An HTML report server only", "A Python class generator"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["pytest","Test function","requests"],["Fixture","page","provides"],["Browser","Context + tab","test"],["Teardown","Cleanup","managed"]], links: ["injects","uses","closes"], caption: "Pytest injects a managed page fixture and cleans up after the test." } },
      { title: "Fixtures, hooks & test isolation", category: "TESTS & FIXTURES", duration: 8, summary: "Share setup safely without making tests depend on one another.", theory: "Fixtures provide reusable setup and teardown, with scopes such as function, class, module, and session. Use the narrowest scope that is safe for the state involved. A fresh page/context per test isolates cookies and storage. Tests should be independently runnable and should not depend on execution order.", analogy: "Isolation gives each test a clean room. Shared setup can save time, but shared mutable state lets one test contaminate another.", goal: "Choose a fixture scope and explain how browser-context isolation prevents leaks.", code: "import pytest\nfrom playwright.sync_api import Page\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef signed_in_page(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://example.com/login\")\n    page.get_by_label(\"Email\").fill(\"test@example.com\")\n    page.get_by_label(\"Password\").fill(\"secret\")\n    page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Sign in\").click()\n    return page", output: "A fixture composes reusable setup; each test can still receive an isolated page.", quiz: { question: "What is usually the safest fixture scope for mutable browser state?", options: ["Function scope", "One shared global page for every run", "No cleanup"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Fixture","Setup","per test"],["Context","Fresh state","cookies"],["Test","Actions","independent"],["Teardown","Close","cleanup"]], links: ["creates","isolates","ends with"], caption: "Function-scoped pages help tests start with clean browser state." } },
      { title: "Navigation, waits & timeouts", category: "TESTS & FIXTURES", duration: 7, summary: "Wait for the condition that matters instead of guessing with sleeps.", theory: "Navigation methods support wait_until states such as commit, domcontentloaded, load, and networkidle; choose according to application behavior. Locator actions and web-first assertions already wait for conditions. Fixed sleeps slow suites and are unreliable. Configure default or per-operation timeouts based on realistic application response times.", analogy: "Wait for the specific event you need, not an arbitrary number of seconds and a hope that the page is ready.", goal: "Choose a meaningful navigation state and avoid unnecessary fixed sleeps.", code: "page.goto(\n    \"https://example.com\",\n    wait_until=\"domcontentloaded\"\n)\npage.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Load results\").click()\nexpect(page.get_by_role(\"listitem\")).to_have_count(3)", output: "Navigate to a useful milestone, then wait for the actual UI condition.", quiz: { question: "Why are fixed sleeps usually a poor synchronization strategy?", options: ["They are both slow and timing-dependent", "They assert the correct UI state", "They make locator queries semantic"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Navigate","Milestone","DOMContentLoaded"],["Interact","Action","trigger"],["Expect","UI condition","retry"],["Continue","Ready","test"]], links: ["reaches","starts","waits for"], caption: "Synchronization should target observable application state, not elapsed time." } },
      { title: "Forms, dialogs & file uploads", category: "TESTS & FIXTURES", duration: 7, summary: "Automate common form controls, browser dialogs, and uploads.", theory: "Use label- or role-based locators for forms, then choose fill, check, select_option, or set_input_files. Handle JavaScript alert, confirm, and prompt dialogs with the page dialog event before triggering them. For uploads, pass a file path or file payload. Prefer temporary test files and clean them up.", analogy: "Treat each browser control according to its purpose; dialogs are events to handle, and uploads are input data to provide.", goal: "Select Playwright APIs for form fields, dialogs, and upload inputs.", code: "page.get_by_label(\"Name\").fill(\"Ari\")\npage.get_by_label(\"Terms\").check()\npage.get_by_label(\"Resume\").set_input_files(\"resume.pdf\")\n\npage.once(\"dialog\", lambda dialog: dialog.accept())\npage.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Delete\").click()", output: "Fill fields, supply a file, and register the dialog handler before clicking.", quiz: { question: "When should a dialog handler be registered?", options: ["Before the action that triggers the dialog", "After the dialog is dismissed", "Only after closing the browser"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Form","Fill / check","input"],["Upload","set_input_files","file"],["Dialog","handler","event"],["Submit","assert","result"]], links: ["provides","selects","handles","verifies"], caption: "Prepare event handlers before triggering actions that emit browser events." } },
      { title: "Alerts, confirms & prompt dialogs", category: "TESTS & FIXTURES", duration: 6, summary: "Handle JavaScript alert, confirm, and prompt dialogs before they block the page.", theory: "A JavaScript alert is acknowledged with dialog.accept(). A confirm dialog can be accepted or dismissed, and a prompt can be accepted with input text or dismissed. Register page.once(\"dialog\", handler) before the action that opens the dialog, then assert dialog.type, dialog.message, or dialog.default_value within the handler. An unhandled dialog can pause browser actions.", analogy: "Set the receptionist's instructions before triggering the doorbell: alert means acknowledge, confirm means accept or cancel, and prompt means answer or cancel.", goal: "Choose the right accept/dismiss behavior for alert, confirm, and prompt dialog types.", code: "dialogs = []\n\ndef handle_dialog(dialog):\n    dialogs.append((dialog.type, dialog.message))\n    if dialog.type == \"prompt\":\n        dialog.accept(\"Asha\")\n    elif dialog.type == \"confirm\":\n        dialog.dismiss()\n    else:  # alert\n        dialog.accept()\n\npage.on(\"dialog\", handle_dialog)\npage.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Show alert\").click()\npage.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Ask name\").click()", output: "Listen first → inspect dialog type → accept or dismiss → continue.", quiz: { question: "What should happen before clicking a button that opens a dialog?", options: ["Register a dialog event handler", "Call accept after the click returns", "Wait for an arbitrary timeout"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Register","page.on(\"dialog\")","listen first"],["Inspect","alert · confirm · prompt","type + message"],["Decide","accept / dismiss","handle"],["Browser","continues","assert result"]], links: ["before trigger","identifies","chooses","resumes"], caption: "The event handler must be ready before a modal dialog interrupts the page." } },
      { title: "Frames, popups & downloads", category: "TESTS & FIXTURES", duration: 8, summary: "Work with iframes and capture related pages and downloads.", theory: "Use frame_locator to locate content inside an iframe. For a popup or download, register the corresponding expect_popup or expect_download event before the action that triggers it; then inspect the captured object. This prevents races where the event happens before the test starts waiting.", analogy: "A popup and a download are events that can happen quickly: set out the catcher first, then trigger the action.", goal: "Interact with a frame and capture a popup or download safely.", code: "with page.expect_popup() as popup_info:\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Open report\").click()\nreport_page = popup_info.value\n\nwith page.expect_download() as download_info:\n    page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Download CSV\").click()\ndownload = download_info.value", output: "Register the event wait before clicking; inspect the event result afterward.", quiz: { question: "Why use expect_popup before clicking the popup link?", options: ["It avoids missing the fast popup event", "It prevents the popup from opening", "It converts the link into an iframe"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Register wait","expect_popup","listen"],["Click link","User action","trigger"],["Popup","New page","captured"],["Inspect","Assertions","verify"]], links: ["before","emits","then"], caption: "Listen for the event before triggering it so no event is missed." } }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "Reliable automation & delivery", symbol: "↗", lessons: [
      { title: "Network requests & API testing", category: "RELIABILITY & DELIVERY", duration: 8, summary: "Observe network traffic, mock a response, and call APIs directly.", theory: "Playwright can monitor requests and responses, wait for a matching response, and intercept or fulfill routes for deterministic tests. APIRequestContext supports API checks without a browser page and can share authentication state. Keep mocks focused; use separate tests for real integration with backend services.", analogy: "The browser is a busy street: listen for the specific request you expect, or set a controlled detour when testing a UI state.", goal: "Explain the difference between observing, mocking, and directly testing an API.", code: "with page.expect_response(\n    lambda response: \"/api/items\" in response.url\n) as response_info:\n    page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Refresh\").click()\n\nresponse = response_info.value\nassert response.ok", output: "Wait for the matching response before triggering the request.", quiz: { question: "When should `expect_response` be entered?", options: ["Before the action that sends the request", "After the response has already arrived", "Only during browser installation"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["UI action","Refresh","trigger"],["Request","/api/items","network"],["Response","status","capture"],["Assert","ok","result"]], links: ["sends","returns","checks"], caption: "Observe a targeted network response and assert its status or payload." } },
      { title: "Authentication & storage state", category: "RELIABILITY & DELIVERY", duration: 7, summary: "Reuse authenticated setup safely with browser storage state.", theory: "A browser context can save cookies and local storage to a storage-state file, then create authenticated contexts from it. Generate state through a trusted test setup, keep the file out of version control because it may contain credentials, and refresh it when authentication changes. Isolate accounts when tests modify server-side data.", analogy: "Storage state is a spare key to a test account: useful for setup, but it must be protected and kept current.", goal: "Describe how storage state helps reuse login and why it must be protected.", code: "# Save state after logging in\ncontext.storage_state(path=\".auth/user.json\")\n\n# Reuse it in a fresh context\ncontext = browser.new_context(\n    storage_state=\".auth/user.json\"\n)", output: "A new context starts with saved cookies and local storage.", quiz: { question: "Why should a storage-state file be excluded from source control?", options: ["It may contain reusable authentication credentials", "It contains only harmless screenshots", "Playwright cannot read tracked files"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Login","Test account","authenticate"],["Storage state","Cookies + local storage","save"],["Fresh context","Restore","reuse session"],["Secret handling","Protect","never commit"]], links: ["creates","loads into","requires"], caption: "Saved browser state can speed setup, but may contain sensitive credentials." } },
      { title: "Trace viewer, screenshots & debugging", category: "RELIABILITY & DELIVERY", duration: 7, summary: "Use traces and artifacts to diagnose failed or flaky tests.", theory: "Playwright traces can include action history, DOM snapshots, screenshots, and network details. Configure tracing in the test runner or record it around a scenario, then inspect it with the trace viewer. Capture screenshots or video selectively, especially on failure, to balance debugging value against storage and privacy.", analogy: "A trace is a flight recorder for a test: it helps reconstruct what the browser saw and did before failure.", goal: "Explain what a trace captures and how it helps debug failures.", code: "# pytest-playwright options:\n# pytest --tracing retain-on-failure\n# pytest --screenshot only-on-failure\n\n# Open a saved trace\npython -m playwright show-trace trace.zip", output: "Inspect the trace timeline, DOM snapshots, screenshots, and network.", quiz: { question: "What is a Playwright trace especially useful for?", options: ["Reconstructing browser actions and state around failure", "Replacing all test assertions", "Installing Python packages"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Test","Actions","timeline"],["Trace","DOM + network","record"],["Failure","Context","evidence"],["Viewer","Inspect","debug"]], links: ["records","preserves","opens in"], caption: "Trace artifacts preserve details that help explain a failure after the run." } },
      { title: "Parallelism, browsers & configuration", category: "RELIABILITY & DELIVERY", duration: 8, summary: "Run across engines and workers without sharing unsafe state.", theory: "Run tests across Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit to find engine-specific issues. Parallel execution improves throughput but requires tests to avoid shared mutable accounts, files, and server state. Use pytest-xdist or CI sharding where appropriate, and configure base URLs, timeouts, retries, and browser projects explicitly.", analogy: "Parallel workers are separate lanes: they go faster when each has independent test data and does not block the others.", goal: "Identify requirements for safe parallel tests and cross-browser coverage.", code: "# pytest-playwright supports browser selection:\n# pytest --browser chromium\n# pytest --browser firefox\n# pytest --browser webkit\n\n# Parallel workers can use pytest-xdist:\n# pytest -n 4", output: "Parallelize only when tests and their data are safe to run independently.", quiz: { question: "What is a key requirement for reliable parallel tests?", options: ["Independent test data and isolated state", "One shared mutable account for all workers", "Random sleeps in each test"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Test suite","Cases","split"],["Worker 1","Browser A","isolated"],["Worker 2","Browser B","isolated"],["Merge","Results","report"]], links: ["distributes to","runs independently","combines"], caption: "Parallel workers need isolated state and a combined result report." } },
      { title: "CI, flakiness & interview strategy", category: "RELIABILITY & DELIVERY", duration: 8, summary: "Deliver stable tests in CI and explain design choices in interviews.", theory: "A reliable CI job installs pinned project dependencies, installs the required browsers, runs tests with useful reports, and uploads failure artifacts such as traces. Reduce flakiness by using semantic locators, web-first assertions, isolated state, and deterministic test data—not by adding blanket retries. In interviews, explain the trade-off and how you would verify it.", analogy: "A strong test suite is a dependable smoke alarm: sensitive to real problems, quiet during normal operation, and informative when it fires.", goal: "Outline a CI workflow and explain how to reduce flakiness at its source.", code: "# Typical CI steps\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\npython -m playwright install --with-deps\npytest --tracing retain-on-failure\n\n# Publish test reports and failure traces as CI artifacts", output: "Reproducible install → browser setup → tests → actionable artifacts", quiz: { question: "What is the best first response to a flaky Playwright test?", options: ["Find and fix the synchronization or state issue", "Add a long fixed sleep", "Retry forever and hide the failure"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Install","Pinned deps","repeatable"],["Browsers","Install engines","ready"],["pytest","Run suite","result"],["Artifacts","Trace + report","diagnose"]], links: ["then","then","publish"], caption: "A useful CI pipeline is reproducible and preserves evidence when tests fail." } }
    ]
  }
];

chapters.splice(4, 0, {
  title: "Python test engineering", symbol: "⚙", lessons: [
    { title: "Playwright compared with other tools", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 6, summary: "Choose Playwright, Selenium, Cypress, or Puppeteer for the right job.", theory: "All four tools automate browsers, but their ecosystems and trade-offs differ. Playwright supports Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit with Python, JavaScript/TypeScript, Java, and .NET APIs, plus isolated browser contexts and auto-waiting. Selenium has a broad WebDriver ecosystem, language reach, and mature grid support. Cypress emphasizes its JavaScript/TypeScript developer workflow and component/E2E testing. Puppeteer is a JavaScript library focused mainly on Chromium-based automation. Compare the actual browser, language, architecture, and test-runner needs before choosing; they are not interchangeable in every environment.", analogy: "Choose a workshop tool by the material and job: browser support, language, test runner, and deployment environment matter more than a popularity ranking.", goal: "Name meaningful differences between Playwright, Selenium, Cypress, and Puppeteer.", code: "# Playwright Python: pytest + Python APIs\n# Selenium: WebDriver ecosystem, broad language/grid support\n# Cypress: JavaScript/TypeScript-centered test runner\n# Puppeteer: JavaScript browser automation, Chromium-focused\n\n# Playwright can drive multiple engines:\n# python -m playwright install chromium firefox webkit", output: "Compare language, engine coverage, runner, isolation, and ecosystem before selecting.", quiz: { question: "Which comparison is most accurate?", options: ["Playwright supports Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit with Python APIs", "Puppeteer is the Python test runner built into pytest", "Selenium and Cypress have identical APIs and browser architectures"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Need","Language + runner","requirements"],["Compare","Engines + isolation","trade-offs"],["Choose","Tool + ecosystem","fit"],["Verify","Target environment","coverage"]], links: ["defines","evaluates","validate in"], caption: "Select a browser automation tool by matching concrete project requirements." } },
    { title: "Python project setup & layout", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 7, summary: "Create a maintainable pytest-playwright project and install its browsers.", theory: "This course uses Playwright for Python with pytest, not the Node.js Playwright Test runner. Therefore `npm init playwright@latest`, `npx playwright install`, and a TypeScript `playwright.config.ts` belong to the JavaScript/TypeScript setup. In Python, install pytest-playwright with pip, then install browser binaries using `python -m playwright install`. Keep tests, shared fixtures, requirements, and pytest configuration in predictable locations; commit dependency declarations, not virtual environments or authentication secrets.", analogy: "The package is your controller, browser binaries are the engines, and the project layout keeps tests and shared setup easy to find.", goal: "Set up the Python test environment and distinguish it from the Node.js workflow.", code: "# Create and activate a virtual environment, then:\npython -m pip install pytest-playwright\npython -m playwright install\n\n# Example project layout:\n# project/\n#   pytest.ini\n#   requirements.txt\n#   conftest.py\n#   tests/\n#     test_home.py\n#   .gitignore", output: "Python setup: install pytest-playwright → install browsers → run pytest.", quiz: { question: "Which command installs Playwright browser binaries in the Python workflow?", options: ["python -m playwright install", "npx playwright install", "npm init playwright@latest"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Python environment","pytest-playwright","dependencies"],["Browser install","Chromium / Firefox / WebKit","engines"],["Project tree","tests + conftest.py","organized"],["pytest","Discover tests","run"]], links: ["installs","organizes","executes"], caption: "Use Python package and pytest commands in this Python course." } },
    { title: "Pytest test anatomy, fixtures & hooks", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 8, summary: "Structure tests and use pytest fixtures for setup, teardown, and browser lifecycle.", theory: "Pytest discovers test_*.py files and test_* functions (or methods on Test* classes). The pytest-playwright plugin supplies managed page, context, browser, and browser_name fixtures. A fixture can arrange state, yield the resource, then clean up; conftest.py shares fixtures within a test directory. pytest.mark.parametrize and test classes provide Python patterns for grouping and data sets. JavaScript's test(), test.describe(), beforeEach, and afterEach are not Python APIs: use pytest fixtures and documented pytest hooks when appropriate. Prefer per-test browser state unless sharing is demonstrably safe.", analogy: "A fixture prepares a clean test room and tears it down afterward; the test itself describes one behavior to verify.", goal: "Write a pytest test with managed browser fixtures and teardown.", code: "# conftest.py\nimport pytest\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef signed_in_page(page):\n    page.goto(\"https://example.com/login\")\n    page.get_by_label(\"Email\").fill(\"learner@example.com\")\n    page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Sign in\").click()\n    yield page\n    # Per-test cleanup can go here.\n\n# tests/test_account.py\ndef test_account_heading(signed_in_page):\n    assert signed_in_page.get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Account\").is_visible()", output: "Pytest setup fixture → test function → fixture teardown.", quiz: { question: "What is the Python pytest equivalent of reusable before/after setup?", options: ["A fixture that can yield and clean up afterward", "test.describe() from the JavaScript runner", "A browser sleep in every test"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Fixture setup","Arrange state","before test"],["Managed page","page fixture","isolated"],["Test function","Act + assert","behavior"],["Fixture teardown","after yield","cleanup"]], links: ["provides","runs in","cleans after"], caption: "Pytest fixtures wrap test work with reusable setup and teardown." } },
    { title: "pytest.ini configuration & browser matrix", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 8, summary: "Configure pytest defaults, browser runs, timeouts, and report plugins.", theory: "Python Playwright projects use pytest configuration rather than playwright.config.ts. Put pytest options in pytest.ini or pyproject.toml: addopts can set browser selection, tracing, screenshots, and video, while pytest-playwright's base_url support uses pytest-base-url. Run `--browser` multiple times for a cross-browser matrix; this is the practical pytest equivalent of projects, not a TypeScript projects block. Pytest's built-in terminal output is a reporter; install pytest-html, pytest-json-report, or allure-pytest when those report formats are needed. A test timeout requires a pytest timeout plugin; distinguish it from Playwright action and assertion timeouts.", analogy: "A configuration file is a reusable launch checklist: it sets sensible defaults while command-line options can override them for one run.", goal: "Configure Python pytest defaults and explain multi-browser runs and reporter plugins.", code: "# pytest.ini\n[pytest]\ntestpaths = tests\naddopts = --browser chromium --tracing retain-on-failure --screenshot only-on-failure --video retain-on-failure\n\n# Optional base URL (install pytest-base-url)\n# base_url = https://staging.example.test\n\n# Multi-browser run; pytest-playwright accepts --browser repeatedly:\n# pytest --browser chromium --browser firefox --browser webkit\n# Optional reporters: pip install pytest-html pytest-json-report allure-pytest", output: "pytest.ini + pytest-playwright options configure runs; plugins add optional report formats.", quiz: { question: "What is the pytest-playwright counterpart to choosing multiple browser projects?", options: ["Pass --browser for each engine in the pytest run", "Create a TypeScript playwright.config.ts", "Use npx playwright test"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["pytest.ini","testpaths + addopts","defaults"],["Browser flags","Chromium / Firefox / WebKit","matrix"],["Artifacts","trace + screenshot + video","failure evidence"],["Reporter plugin","HTML / JSON / Allure","format"]], links: ["configures","selects","publishes as"], caption: "The Python runner uses pytest configuration and plugins rather than a TS config." } },
    { title: "Run, select & debug pytest tests", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 7, summary: "Run the suite, filter tests, open a visible browser, and debug failures.", theory: "Use `pytest` to run tests, `pytest -v` for names, `pytest --headed` to see the browser, and repeated `--browser` options for engine coverage. Filter with pytest's `-k` expression (not Playwright Test's `--grep`); use `--collect-only` to inspect discovery and `--pdb` to enter Python's debugger after a failure. `--trace` enters the debugger before each test. pytest-playwright does not provide the JavaScript runner's `--ui` mode or `--debug` flag; use pytest plus a Python debugger or IDE instead. `pytest --last-failed` can rerun failures when the cache plugin is enabled.", analogy: "Run the whole checklist first, then use pytest's own filters and debugger to zoom in on one failing behavior.", goal: "Choose correct pytest commands for normal, headed, filtered, and debug runs.", code: "# Run all discovered tests\npytest -q\n\n# Verbose, headed, and cross-browser\npytest -v --headed --browser chromium --browser firefox\n\n# Select tests by keyword; inspect collection; debug failure\npytest -k \"login and not slow\" --collect-only\npytest --pdb tests/test_login.py::test_valid_login", output: "Use pytest CLI flags; `--grep`, `--ui`, and `--debug` are not pytest-playwright commands.", quiz: { question: "How do you filter pytest tests by a keyword expression?", options: ["pytest -k \"login and not slow\"", "npx playwright test --grep login", "pytest --ui"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Discover","pytest","collect"],["Select","-k expression","filter"],["Run","--headed / --browser","execute"],["Debug","--pdb","inspect"]], links: ["collects","narrows to","runs and diagnoses"], caption: "Python test execution uses pytest's discovery, selection, and debug options." } },
    { title: "Assertions: web-first, soft & screenshots", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 8, summary: "Assert text, values, attributes, counts, URLs, and screenshots reliably.", theory: "Import `expect` from playwright.sync_api and use web-first assertions such as to_be_visible, to_have_text, to_contain_text, to_have_value, to_have_attribute, to_have_count, and page URL assertions. These retry until success or timeout. A regular Python `assert locator.is_visible()` evaluates once and does not retry; use it for already-computed Python values, not eventual UI state. `expect.soft` records failures and continues, but Playwright documents soft assertions as supported with pytest-playwright version 0.8.0 or newer. Screenshot assertions use `expect(page).to_have_screenshot()` and may require approved baselines; review visual diffs and avoid unstable dynamic regions.", analogy: "A web-first assertion keeps checking the live page until it matches; a plain assert is a single snapshot in time.", goal: "Select retrying assertions for changing UI and identify when soft/screenshot assertions apply.", code: "from playwright.sync_api import expect\n\nexpect(page.get_by_role(\"heading\")).to_have_text(\"Welcome\")\nexpect(page.get_by_label(\"Email\")).to_have_value(\"learner@example.com\")\nexpect(page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Save\")).to_have_attribute(\"type\", \"submit\")\nexpect(page.get_by_role(\"row\")).to_have_count(3)\nexpect(page).to_have_screenshot(\"dashboard.png\")\n\n# pytest-playwright >= 0.8.0\nexpect.soft(page.get_by_test_id(\"status\")).to_have_text(\"Ready\")", output: "Web-first assertions retry; screenshot checks compare baselines; soft failures allow later checks to run.", quiz: { question: "Which assertion is best when text may appear after an asynchronous UI update?", options: ["expect(locator).to_have_text(expected)", "assert locator.text_content() == expected immediately", "time.sleep(5) followed by no assertion"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["UI state","May update later","async"],["expect(locator)","Retrying check","web-first"],["Screenshot","Compare baseline","visual diff"],["Result","pass / reported failure","clear"]], links: ["observed by","can compare","reports"], caption: "Choose retrying assertions for UI state and snapshots for visual regression." } },
    { title: "Locator chaining, filtering & strict mode", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 8, summary: "Build precise locators and diagnose ambiguous matches instead of guessing.", theory: "Prefer accessible/user-facing locators: get_by_role with an accessible name, get_by_label for fields, get_by_placeholder where appropriate, get_by_text for content, and get_by_test_id for an explicit test contract. Scope with locator chaining and filter(has_text=..., has=...). Use first, last, or nth(index) only when position is intentional. Locator.and_() intersects two locators; locator.or_() matches either and can itself produce multiple matches. Actions that require one element use strict matching and fail when several elements match—that error usually signals an ambiguous locator, not a reason to blindly choose the first. CSS and XPath are fallbacks when semantic locators are unsuitable.", analogy: "A precise locator is a full address; strict mode refuses to act when your address names several houses.", goal: "Resolve duplicate locator matches using scope and user-facing semantics.", code: "card = page.locator(\".card\").filter(has_text=\"Price: $25\")\nbutton = card.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Add to cart\")\nbutton.click()\n\nitems = page.get_by_role(\"listitem\")\nitems.first.click()       # first is intentional here\nthird = items.nth(2)      # zero-based index\n\n# Python method names use underscores:\nready_button = page.get_by_role(\"button\").and_(\n    page.locator(\"[data-ready='true']\")\n)", output: "Scope → filter → verify uniqueness → act; use positional selection only by design.", quiz: { question: "What does a strict-mode violation usually indicate for an action locator?", options: ["The locator matched more than one element and needs disambiguation", "The browser engine is unsupported", "The page needs a fixed sleep"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Broad query","many elements","ambiguous"],["Scope","card / region","narrow"],["Filter","text / has","precise"],["Strict action","one target","safe"]], links: ["reveals","narrows to","requires"], caption: "Locator strictness helps expose ambiguous selectors before the wrong element is clicked." } },
    { title: "Actions & actionability checks", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 8, summary: "Use user-like locator actions and understand what Playwright waits for.", theory: "Use the action matching the interaction: click, dblclick, fill, press, check/uncheck, hover, drag_to, scroll_into_view_if_needed, select_option, and set_input_files. For character-by-character entry, Python's press_sequentially can be used when the page depends on key events; fill is usually the more resilient default. Before actions such as click, Playwright waits for a unique target and applicable actionability checks: visible, stable, enabled, and receiving pointer events. Filling also requires an editable target. Do not use force to hide a real overlay or disabled-state problem unless deliberately testing that behavior.", analogy: "Before touching a control, Playwright checks that it is the one you asked for, visible, settled, enabled, and not covered.", goal: "Pick the right locator action and explain click actionability checks.", code: "search = page.get_by_placeholder(\"Search\")\nsearch.fill(\"Playwright\")\nsearch.press(\"Enter\")\n\npage.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"More options\").dblclick()\nsource.drag_to(target)\npage.get_by_label(\"Country\").select_option(label=\"Canada\")\npage.get_by_label(\"Resume\").set_input_files(\"resume.pdf\")\ntarget.scroll_into_view_if_needed()", output: "Actionability waits for a real user-like interaction instead of clicking an unstable or obscured target.", quiz: { question: "Which is an actionability check for locator.click()?", options: ["The target receives pointer events and is stable", "The page has no JavaScript loaded", "The test ran in headless mode"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Locate","unique target","resolve"],["Check","visible · stable · enabled","ready"],["Hit test","receives events","uncovered"],["Act","click / fill / drag","interact"]], links: ["selects","verifies","allows"], caption: "Playwright waits for the target and required actionability conditions before acting." } },
    { title: "Explicit waits & timeout budgets", category: "TEST ENGINEERING", duration: 8, summary: "Wait for the exact condition needed and configure each timeout layer deliberately.", theory: "Prefer locator actions and web-first assertions because they retry automatically. When an explicit wait is needed, use locator.wait_for(state=\"visible\"), page.wait_for_load_state(), or page.wait_for_function() for the actual condition. `page.wait_for_selector()` remains available but locator-based waits are preferred; fixed `wait_for_timeout()` sleeps are a last resort. Python does not provide JavaScript Playwright Test's `expect.poll`; poll a locator assertion or use an explicit predicate wait. Action timeout can be set per action or with page.set_default_timeout; navigation has page.set_default_navigation_timeout; assertion timeout uses expect.set_options or a per-assertion timeout. Pytest itself has no built-in test timeout: use a timeout plugin if a whole-test deadline is needed.", analogy: "Set separate deadlines for clicking, navigating, asserting, and the entire test; one generous timeout should not mask every delay.", goal: "Choose condition-based waits and distinguish action, navigation, assertion, and test timeouts.", code: "from playwright.sync_api import expect\n\npage.set_default_timeout(5_000)             # actions\npage.set_default_navigation_timeout(15_000) # navigation\npage.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Load\").click(timeout=8_000)\npage.wait_for_load_state(\"domcontentloaded\")\npage.get_by_test_id(\"results\").wait_for(state=\"visible\")\nexpect(page.get_by_role(\"listitem\")).to_have_count(3, timeout=10_000)\n\n# Configure a whole-test deadline with a pytest timeout plugin,\n# not a Playwright locator timeout.", output: "Wait for a state or event; configure the timeout layer that owns that operation.", quiz: { question: "Which is the preferred replacement for a fixed sleep while waiting for a locator to appear?", options: ["locator.wait_for(state=\"visible\") or a web-first expect assertion", "page.wait_for_timeout(5000) in every test", "A bigger test timeout with no condition"], answer: 0 }, visual: { nodes: [["Condition","visible / loaded","target"],["Wait API","locator / page","observe"],["Timeout layer","action / nav / expect","bounded"],["Continue","ready or error","result"]], links: ["defines","waits on","ends as"], caption: "Condition-based waits and operation-specific timeouts make failures understandable." } }
  ]
});

